/* Domain 32 — Threat Intelligence, OSINT & Social Engineering Defense (facts verified October 2026).
   Everything here is taught for authorized security work, journalism and research. Exercises target the learner's own
   footprint, fictional personas, public companies' public assets, or sanctioned challenges (Trace Labs rules, OSINT Dojo,
   Gralhix exercises). Nothing in this pack teaches how to locate, profile or surveil a private individual. */
BOOTCAMP.phases.push({
id:"p32", num:32, title:"Threat Intelligence, OSINT & Social Engineering Defense", short:"Threat Intel",
tagline:"Turn public information into decisions: run the intelligence cycle, collect open sources legally and safely, verify images and claims, map adversaries with ATT&CK and the Diamond Model, and defend people against the manipulation that opens most breaches.",
prereq:"Domains 2 (networking: DNS, TLS, IP addressing), 3 (Linux: shell, Docker), 7 (cybersecurity: threats, risk, the human factor) and 8 (pentest methodology, rules of engagement). Domain 21 (blue team) and Domain 47 (dark web) are siblings you will cross into. Every exercise is authorized, passive and people-free unless it is your own footprint.",
outcomes:[
"Run the intelligence cycle end to end: write priority intelligence requirements, plan collection, process and analyze with structured techniques, and disseminate with estimative language and stated confidence.",
"Define open-source intelligence precisely, separate passive from active collection, and state the legal lines (CFAA, state harassment law, GDPR/CCPA/Texas TDPSA, FCRA, platform terms of service) and the ethics that govern every collection decision.",
"Set up and operate an OSINT workstation with analyst OPSEC: isolated VM and browser profiles, research accounts used within policy, evidence capture with timestamps and hashes, and a source log graded with the Admiralty code.",
"Map a company's external attack surface from public data alone (WHOIS/RDAP, passive DNS, certificate transparency, Shodan/Censys, code and cloud exposure, breach-data awareness), feed it into an authorized recon phase and into a blue team's reduction plan.",
"Verify images and video: reverse image search, EXIF metadata, geolocation from landmarks and shadows, chronolocation arithmetic with SunCalc, keyframe analysis and provenance checks for synthetic media.",
"Operate threat intelligence tooling: tell IOCs from TTPs, map reports to MITRE ATT&CK, draw a Diamond Model, read and write STIX 2.1, run MISP in Docker, judge feed quality and profile threat actors from public reporting.",
"Recognize and defend against social engineering: the psychology, every major vector, business email compromise case studies, awareness programs and authorized phishing simulations that measure the right things.",
"Write and brief an intelligence product in BLUF form with TLP marking, measure an intel program, and map the GIAC GOSI objectives to a concrete study plan."
],
texts:["Richards J. Heuer Jr., Psychology of Intelligence Analysis (CIA Center for the Study of Intelligence, 1999; free PDF)","Michael Bazzell, OSINT Techniques: Resources for Uncovering Online Information, 11th edition (2024)","Caltagirone, Pendergast & Betz, The Diamond Model of Intrusion Analysis (2013) and the MITRE ATT&CK knowledge base (attack.mitre.org)","Bellingcat's Online Investigation Toolkit and how-to guides (bellingcat.com/resources); the Berkeley Protocol on Digital Open Source Investigations (OHCHR/UC Berkeley Human Rights Center, launched 2020, UN edition 2022)"],
modules:[
{id:"p32m1", title:"The Intelligence Cycle: From Question to Decision", minutes:50,
outcomes:["Name and explain the six stages of the intelligence cycle and what goes wrong when one is skipped.","Distinguish strategic, operational and tactical intelligence by consumer, horizon and product.","Write a priority intelligence requirement that can actually be answered and decomposed into collection tasks.","Apply analytic standards: estimative language, confidence levels, the Admiralty code and Analysis of Competing Hypotheses.","Place an intelligence function inside a SOC and alongside a red team."],
body:`<p>The question this module answers: how does a pile of facts become something a decision-maker can act on, and why must a penetration tester or SOC analyst care? Because facts are never the scarce resource; any analyst can collect a thousand indicators before lunch. What a company pays for is a defensible judgment, delivered in time, to the person who can change something. <strong>Threat intelligence</strong> is exactly that: evidence-based knowledge about an adversary or hazard, with context and a recommendation, produced for a specific consumer. Everything else in this domain is a collection or analysis technique in service of this loop.</p>
<h4>The six stages</h4>
<p>The <strong>intelligence cycle</strong> is the oldest model in the trade and still the one every certification tests. Six stages (U.S. doctrine in JP 2-0 uses the same six; some agencies compress them to five): <em>planning and direction</em>, where a consumer states what they need; <em>collection</em>, gathering raw data against those needs; <em>processing</em>, turning raw data into usable form (translation, parsing, normalization, deduplication, decoding); <em>analysis and production</em>, where humans or models interpret the processed data and write a product; <em>dissemination</em>, delivering it to the consumer in a form they can use; and <em>feedback</em>, learning whether it helped and refining the requirements. It is drawn as a circle because feedback rewrites direction. The classic failure is a team that lives in collection and production and never asks for direction: beautiful reports nobody requested, and the budget disappears within two years.</p>
<table><tr><th>Level</th><th>Consumer</th><th>Horizon</th><th>Typical question</th><th>Product</th></tr>
<tr><td>Strategic</td><td>CISO, board, risk committee</td><td>Quarters to years</td><td>Which actors target our sector and why? What does the next regulation change?</td><td>Threat landscape assessment, annual risk brief</td></tr>
<tr><td>Operational</td><td>Security managers, IR lead, red team</td><td>Weeks to months</td><td>How does this ransomware crew get in and move? What should our next tabletop assume?</td><td>Campaign report, actor profile, ATT&CK heat map</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tactical</td><td>SOC analysts, detection engineers, firewall admins</td><td>Hours to days</td><td>Which hashes, domains and behaviors should we block or hunt for today?</td><td>IOC feed, Sigma/YARA rules, hunt hypotheses</td></tr></table>
<h4>Requirements: the PIR</h4>
<p>Direction is written as requirements. A <strong>PIR</strong>, a priority intelligence requirement, is a question the decision-maker needs answered, phrased so you can tell when it has been answered and tied to a decision they will make. "Tell me about ransomware" is not a PIR. "Which ransomware groups have hit U.S. regional telecom contractors in the last twelve months, through what initial access, and does our remote-access stack appear in their tradecraft?" is a PIR, because it names a population, a window, a mechanism and the decision it informs (what to harden first). A PIR decomposes into specific intelligence requirements (SIRs): "list public incidents at telecom contractors since October 2025"; "extract initial-access techniques from each"; "compare with our exposed services." Each SIR maps to sources in a <em>collection plan</em>: vendor reports, CISA advisories, leak-site trackers, court filings, the company's own asset inventory. Writing the plan before collecting separates intelligence from browsing.</p>
<h4>Analytic standards and estimative language</h4>
<p>The U.S. Intelligence Community codified analytic tradecraft in ICD 203 (Analytic Standards, 2015): products must be objective, timely, based on all available sources, and must describe sourcing, express uncertainty, distinguish information from assumptions and judgments, consider alternatives and explain changes from earlier judgments. Two tools from that standard travel into every corporate report. <strong>Estimative language</strong> replaces vague words with calibrated ones: <em>almost no chance</em> (1–5%), <em>very unlikely</em> (5–20%), <em>unlikely</em> (20–45%), <em>roughly even chance</em> (45–55%), <em>likely</em> (55–80%), <em>very likely</em> (80–95%), <em>almost certain</em> (95–99%). Then, separately, a <strong>confidence level</strong>, high, moderate or low, describes the quality and corroboration of the evidence behind the judgment. "We assess it is likely (moderate confidence) that the actor used a stolen VPN credential" says two different things: the probability of the claim and how good the evidence is. Mixing them is the most common beginner error in reporting.</p>
<p>Sources get graded too. The <strong>Admiralty code</strong> (the NATO system) rates source reliability A–F (A: completely reliable, E: unreliable, F: cannot be judged) and information credibility 1–6 (1: confirmed by independent sources, 5: improbable, 6: cannot be judged). A vendor report corroborated by a court indictment might be B1; an anonymous forum post is F3 at best. The code forces you to say how much weight a fact can bear before you build a judgment on it.</p>
<h4>Structured analytic techniques and the biases they fight</h4>
<p>Richards Heuer's <em>Psychology of Intelligence Analysis</em> (1999) made the case that the analyst's main enemy is <strong>cognitive bias</strong>: confirmation bias (seeking evidence for the favorite theory), anchoring (over-weighting the first number seen), mirror imaging (assuming the adversary thinks as you do) and availability (the vivid recent case feels likely). Structured analytic techniques are procedures that make the reasoning visible so bias can be caught. The most tested is <strong>ACH</strong>, Analysis of Competing Hypotheses: list every plausible hypothesis, list every piece of evidence, build a matrix and mark each cell consistent (C), inconsistent (I) or neutral (N), then <em>reject hypotheses with the most inconsistencies</em> rather than crowning the one with the most support, because consistent evidence is cheap (it fits several stories) while disconfirming evidence is decisive. Finish with a sensitivity check (which single piece of evidence, if wrong, flips the result?) and milestones that would change your mind. Companions: the Key Assumptions Check, Devil's Advocacy, and the pre-mortem ("it is six months later and we were wrong; why?").</p>
<h4>Where the function sits</h4>
<p>In a SOC (Domain 21) the intelligence team feeds detection engineering with TTP-level knowledge, enriches alerts, prioritizes vulnerabilities with KEV and EPSS context, and briefs leadership. Beside a red team (Domain 8) it produces the scenarios a test should emulate; regulated frameworks such as TIBER-EU and the UK's CBEST require a threat-intelligence phase before the red team may act, so the test imitates the adversaries that actually target the firm. Both share the discipline above: requirements first, graded sources, calibrated judgments, feedback.</p>
<p><em>Exam mapping.</em> GIAC GOSI's "OSINT Methodology" objective and EC-Council CTIA's first three domains (fundamentals, levels, requirements and planning) are this module: the stages in order, the three levels by consumer, PIR decomposition, estimative language versus confidence, and ACH as the named technique for weighing hypotheses.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>An ACH matrix in six minutes.</strong> A regional contractor's VPN appliance started accepting logins from a foreign cloud range at 03:10 local time. Hypotheses: H1, a stolen credential bought from an infostealer log; H2, exploitation of the appliance's unpatched CVE; H3, an employee traveling abroad. Evidence: E1, the account's password was in a breach corpus (C for H1, N for H2, I for H3 only weakly); E2, the appliance firmware was current (N, I, N); E3, the employee was asleep at home per badge and phone records (C, N, I); E4, the login used a browser fingerprint never seen for that user (C, C, I); E5, no exploit artifacts in appliance logs (N, I, N). Count inconsistencies: H1 = 0, H2 = 2, H3 = 3. Judgment: "It is very likely (high confidence) that the session used a stolen credential; exploitation is unlikely." Sensitivity: if E2 is wrong (firmware not actually current), H2 returns. Milestone: a second account from the same cloud range would raise H1 to almost certain. That paragraph, with sources graded, is a tactical product a SOC lead can act on: force a password reset, add the range to the block list, hunt for infostealer malware on the user's machine.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"Intelligence is information.\" Information is raw; intelligence is information that has been evaluated, analyzed against a requirement and delivered with a judgment and a recommendation.","\"The hypothesis with the most supporting evidence wins.\" In ACH you eliminate the hypotheses with the most inconsistencies; supporting evidence is often consistent with several stories at once.","\"Likely and high confidence mean the same thing.\" Likely (55–80%) is a probability of the claim; confidence describes the quality and corroboration of the evidence. A judgment can be likely with low confidence."],
problems:[
{q:"Rewrite 'tell me about phishing threats' as a PIR tied to a decision a mid-size telecom contractor must make, then decompose it into three SIRs.", a:"PIR: Which phishing techniques delivered initial access against U.S. telecom and construction contractors in the last 12 months, and which of them would bypass our current email and MFA controls? Decision: what to fix in Q1. SIRs: (1) list public incidents and vendor reports for the sector since Oct 2025; (2) extract lures and MFA-bypass methods (adversary-in-the-middle kits, push fatigue, help-desk vishing); (3) compare against our controls (DMARC status, MFA type, help-desk verification)."},
{q:"A judgment reads: 'The actor is probably Lazarus (high confidence) because the malware shares a string with a 2019 sample.' Critique it using the standards.", a:"One shared string is weak, easily copied evidence; it supports many hypotheses (code reuse, false flag). High confidence is unjustified; the probability word should be 'possible/roughly even' and confidence low until corroborated by infrastructure, TTP overlap and victimology, with sources graded."},
{q:"Grade these sources with the Admiralty code: (a) a CISA advisory corroborated by two vendors; (b) a single anonymous Telegram claim of a breach; (c) your own firewall logs.", a:"(a) A1 or B1: reliable source, confirmed information. (b) F3 or F4: reliability cannot be judged, credibility possible/doubtful. (c) A1 or A2 for the raw observation (completely reliable source; the interpretation may still be uncertain)."},
{q:"Three hypotheses, seven evidence items. H1 has 5 consistent and 2 inconsistent; H2 has 3 consistent and 0 inconsistent; H3 has 6 consistent and 1 inconsistent. Which survives and why?", a:"H2: it has no inconsistencies. ACH rejects on disconfirmation; H1 and H3 are contradicted by evidence despite more support. Check whether H2's neutral cells hide missing collection."},
{q:"Design the feedback stage for a monthly threat brief: three concrete signals you would collect from consumers.", a:"Which judgments changed a decision (recorded per report), consumer ratings of relevance and timeliness, and follow-up questions asked, which become the next cycle's requirements; add a count of detections or controls traced back to the brief."}
],
checks:[{q:"Six stages of the intelligence cycle?",a:"Planning and direction, collection, processing, analysis and production, dissemination, feedback."},{q:"Percentage band for 'likely' in IC estimative language?",a:"55–80%."},{q:"In ACH, which hypothesis do you reject first?",a:"The one with the most inconsistent evidence."}],
explain:"Explain to ADA why a SOC with no written requirements is not doing intelligence, and walk through an ACH matrix on a small incident, showing where estimative language and confidence enter the final sentence."},

{id:"p32m2", title:"OSINT Foundations, Law, Ethics and Analyst OPSEC", minutes:55,
outcomes:["Define open-source intelligence precisely and separate passive from active collection.","State the legal lines: CFAA after Van Buren, platform terms of service, state harassment and stalking law, GDPR/CCPA/Texas TDPSA and FCRA.","Apply an ethics framework (necessity, proportionality, minimization, no deception of individuals, accountability) to a collection decision.","Build an analyst workstation with isolation, research accounts within policy, and evidence capture with hashes and timestamps.","Audit your own digital footprint and write a cleanup plan."],
body:`<p>The question: what exactly is "open" about open sources, and where does a legal, ethical investigation end and a stalking charge or a felony begin? The answers decide whether you can hold this job. <strong>OSINT</strong>, open-source intelligence, is intelligence produced from publicly available information, collected and analyzed against a requirement and delivered to an audience that needs it (U.S. law, Public Law 109-163 of 2006, defines it almost in those words). "Publicly available" means anyone can lawfully obtain it without deceiving, hacking or paying for stolen goods: websites, public social media, government records, corporate filings, DNS and certificate logs, satellite imagery, archived pages. It does not mean anything a computer can reach. A password-protected page, a private group, a breach dump on a criminal forum, photos visible only after you friend someone: none of that is open source, even though techniques exist to get it.</p>
<h4>Passive versus active collection</h4>
<p><strong>Passive collection</strong> reads what is already published and touches only third-party services: search engines, archives, certificate logs, scan databases such as Shodan that already did the scanning, cached copies. The target never sees you. <em>Active</em> collection touches the target or its people: port scans, directory brute force, DNS zone transfer attempts, friend requests, direct messages, phone calls, account-recovery prompts, "accidentally" liking a post. Active steps change evidence, alert the subject and, without written authorization, cross legal lines. Trace Labs' Search Party rules make the point: passive reconnaissance only, zero contact with the subject or their family and friends, no password resets, logins or registering a subject's email anywhere; violations mean disqualification. Corporate OSINT follows the same split; an authorized penetration test (Domain 8) is where active steps belong, inside the rules of engagement.</p>
<h4>The legal lines</h4>
<ul><li><strong>CFAA</strong> (18 U.S.C. § 1030) criminalizes access "without authorization" or "exceeding authorized access." <em>Van Buren v. United States</em> (2021) narrowed the second phrase to areas of a system you may not enter, not misuse of data you may access; <em>hiQ Labs v. LinkedIn</em> (Ninth Circuit, 2022) held that scraping publicly visible pages likely does not violate the CFAA, yet hiQ still lost on breach of contract later that year. Public pages are generally not a CFAA problem; using someone else's credentials, bypassing a block or guessing at private content is.</li>
<li><strong>Terms of service</strong> are civil, not criminal, but real: most platforms forbid fake accounts, automated scraping and impersonation; breaking them can cost the account, an injunction or damages.</li>
<li><strong>Harassment and stalking law</strong> applies regardless of how public the data was. Texas Penal Code § 42.072 (stalking) and § 42.07 (harassment) punish a pattern of conduct a reasonable person would find threatening or alarming. Aggregating a private person's posts into a dossier, then showing up or making contact, is how OSINT becomes a crime.</li>
<li><strong>Privacy law</strong>: GDPR treats an EU resident's name, photo or handle as personal data needing a documented lawful basis, purpose limitation and minimization; Article 14 even requires telling people when you compile a file on them from third-party sources, with narrow exceptions. California's CCPA/CPRA and the Texas Data Privacy and Security Act (in force since July 1, 2024) regulate businesses that collect and sell personal data. Background information used in hiring falls under the Fair Credit Reporting Act: an employer cannot quietly "OSINT" a candidate instead of running a compliant check.</li>
<li><strong>Stolen data</strong>: downloading breach dumps or buying "logs" is handling stolen credentials; a company checks exposure through services built for it (Have I Been Pwned's domain search), not by hoarding dumps.</li></ul>
<h4>Ethics beyond the law</h4>
<p>Legal is the floor. The Berkeley Protocol on Digital Open Source Investigations (OHCHR and the UC Berkeley Human Rights Center, launched in 2020 and published by the UN in 2022), written for human-rights work, gives the questions any investigation should pass: <em>necessity</em> (does the requirement need this data?), <em>proportionality</em> (is the intrusion proportionate to the harm prevented?), <em>minimization</em> (collect the least, keep it the shortest time, restrict access), <em>verification</em> (never present one unverified source as fact), <em>no deception or engagement</em> with individuals, <em>security</em> of the data and of the people in it, and <em>accountability</em> (a record of who decided what and why). The rule for this curriculum: your targets are your own footprint, fictional personas, organizations' public assets within scope, and sanctioned challenges. You never investigate a private individual, not an ex, not a neighbor, not "just to see." People who break that rule do not stay in the field.</p>
<h4>OPSEC for the analyst</h4>
<p><strong>OPSEC</strong>, operations security, protects you and the investigation: a researcher who browses a suspect domain from the corporate network has just told the adversary who is looking. The baseline workstation: a dedicated virtual machine (a Trace Labs OSINT VM, Kali's OSINT tools or your own Debian build) with snapshots rolled back after each case; browser profiles or containers per investigation with no personal logins; a VPN or, for sensitive work, Tor Browser (Domain 47); a password manager; no personal devices. Research accounts, often called <strong>sock puppet</strong> accounts, need a precise statement: they are personas used to view platforms that hide content from logged-out visitors. Most platforms forbid fake accounts, impersonating a real person can be a crime, and using a persona to befriend or message a private individual is active collection that fails every ethics test above. In authorized corporate work, research accounts exist under written policy, are owned by the organization, never interact with subjects and are documented; in Trace Labs you observe only. Strip EXIF from anything you upload, never reuse usernames across personal and research life, and assume every service logs your IP and device fingerprint.</p>
<h4>Documentation and evidence</h4>
<p>An investigation you cannot reproduce is an opinion. Capture every page as you saw it, with URL and UTC timestamp, and hash the file (SHA-256) to prove later that it was not altered. Hunchly, the investigator's browser extension, automates this, recording every page in a case with hashes and timestamps. Free discipline works too: full-page screenshots named by timestamp, saved HTML, archive.org and archive.today snapshots, and a source log with the Admiralty grade of each item. The case file carries the PIR at the top, the collection plan, findings keyed to sources, and judgments with their confidence: the chain-of-custody habit from Domain 21 forensics applied to the open web.</p>
<h4>Your first lab: your own footprint</h4>
<p>The first target is you. Enumerate your <strong>digital footprint</strong>: every username and email you have used, old forum posts, photos with location data, people-search sites listing your address, breached accounts (Have I Been Pwned by email), public records. Then write the cleanup plan: delete or privatize, rotate reused passwords, enable phishing-resistant MFA, opt out of data brokers with the free workbook Michael Bazzell publishes alongside <em>Extreme Privacy</em>, and set alerts on your name and email. An afternoon on yourself teaches more about how exposure accumulates than any lecture, and you will never again doubt why the field needs ethics.</p>
<p><em>Exam mapping.</em> GOSI's "Privacy and Operational Security Fundamentals" and "OSINT Methodology" objectives are this module: the definition of open source, passive versus active, documenting a fact-based process, and the risks to the investigator and the practices that reduce them. CTIA's planning domain tests the legal and rules-of-engagement side.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>A footprint audit with numbers.</strong> You search your three usernames across a site-enumeration tool and get 41 hits; 11 are you. Have I Been Pwned shows your main email in 6 breaches, 2 with plaintext or weakly hashed passwords, both of which you reused on 4 other sites until today. Google Images on your public profile photo finds 3 copies on sites you never joined (scraped aggregators). Two people-search brokers list your current street and a relative's name. EXIF on 5 photos you posted in 2019 still carries GPS coordinates of your old apartment because that forum did not strip metadata. Cleanup plan, in priority order: rotate the 4 reused passwords and enroll a passkey or security key; delete or privatize the 5 photos; file broker opt-outs (an afternoon with the workbook, follow-ups at 30 days); replace the reused profile photo; set a Google Alert on your name plus city. Total cost: zero dollars and about six hours. Evidence: a case file with screenshots, hashes and the dates each change was made, exactly as you would deliver for a client.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"If I can see it, it's open source.\" Open source means lawfully, publicly available without deception, bypass or stolen goods; content behind a login, a friend request or a breach forum is not.","\"Using a research account is always illegal.\" It is usually a terms-of-service matter; impersonating a real person or using a persona to engage a private individual crosses into illegal or unethical territory. In Trace Labs you never interact at all.","\"Public data means I can build a file on anyone.\" Stalking and harassment statutes judge the conduct and its effect, not the source of the data; GDPR and FCRA add legal duties even to lawful collection."],
problems:[
{q:"Classify each as passive or active and say which needs authorization: (a) reading a company's certificate-transparency entries on crt.sh; (b) running nmap against the hosts you found; (c) viewing a public LinkedIn company page; (d) sending a connection request to an employee from a research account.", a:"(a) passive, no authorization beyond scope; (b) active, needs written authorization (Domain 8 rules of engagement); (c) passive; (d) active engagement with an individual, forbidden in OSINT work and in Trace Labs, and a ToS violation."},
{q:"A hiring manager asks you to 'look into' a candidate's social media before an offer. What law governs and what do you answer?", a:"The Fair Credit Reporting Act governs background information used for employment decisions; informal OSINT risks discrimination claims and FCRA violations. Answer: route through a compliant, consented background-check process; decline the informal search."},
{q:"Design the evidence record for one web page so that a lawyer could rely on it a year later.", a:"Full-page capture (PNG and saved HTML) named with UTC timestamp, the exact URL, SHA-256 of each file recorded in the case log, an archive.org or archive.today snapshot link, the Admiralty grade and the requirement it supports, and who captured it on what workstation."},
{q:"Your employer wants research accounts for brand-protection monitoring. Write the five-line policy that keeps them inside the lines.", a:"Accounts are owned and registered by the company, never impersonate a real person, are used for viewing only (no friending, messaging or posting), are documented with purpose and owner, and are reviewed for platform ToS and legal exposure by counsel before use."},
{q:"Explain why browsing a suspected phishing domain from your office laptop is an OPSEC failure and give the fix.", a:"The domain's logs record the corporate IP and browser fingerprint, tipping off the operator and possibly triggering a targeted response; use the isolated research VM through a VPN or Tor Browser, or a passive source (urlscan.io, VirusTotal) instead of visiting at all."}
],
checks:[{q:"Which Supreme Court case narrowed CFAA's 'exceeds authorized access'?",a:"Van Buren v. United States (2021)."},{q:"Trace Labs' rule on contact with a subject's family?",a:"Zero contact; passive reconnaissance only, or disqualification."},{q:"When did the Texas Data Privacy and Security Act take effect?",a:"July 1, 2024."}],
explain:"Teach ADA the difference between passive and active collection with four examples, then explain why 'public' does not settle the legal or ethical question, naming CFAA, ToS, Texas stalking law and GDPR."},
{id:"p32m3", title:"Corporate Attack-Surface OSINT: Mapping an Organization's Exposure, Legally", minutes:55,
outcomes:["Scope an attack-surface investigation to authorized, people-free targets and name the sources that are passive by construction.","Enumerate domains and hosts from WHOIS/RDAP, passive DNS and certificate transparency, and explain why each exists.","Use Shodan and Censys on assets you own or are authorized to assess, and read what they expose.","Find code, cloud-storage and breach-data exposure without ever touching stolen data.","Hand the map to an authorized recon phase and to a blue team's reduction plan."],
body:`<p>The question: how much of a company can an adversary learn without sending it a single packet, and what does the defender do about it? The answer is the organization's external <strong>attack surface</strong>: every domain, host, service, certificate, repository, bucket and credential that is visible from the outside. Attackers map it first; so should the people defending it. Two rules frame everything in this module. First, <em>authorization and scope</em>: you map assets you own, assets your employer has assigned you in writing, or assets a public bug-bounty policy explicitly places in scope for reconnaissance; otherwise you use a fictional company. Second, <em>people-free</em>: the units of analysis are assets, not employees. You infer an email format to understand exposure; you do not build a list of named staff and their habits. That is the line between attack-surface management and the profiling this curriculum refuses to teach.</p>
<h4>Domains, registration and DNS history</h4>
<p>Start from the legal entity: company filings, trademarks, acquisitions and brand names reveal domains nobody remembers owning. <strong>WHOIS</strong> moved to the structured RDAP protocol: ICANN ended the contractual WHOIS port-43 obligation for gTLD registrars on January 28, 2025, and since GDPR most registrant fields are redacted, so the useful signals are the registrar, creation and expiry dates, name servers and historical records from commercial archives. <strong>Passive DNS</strong> databases (SecurityTrails, DomainTools' DNSDB, VirusTotal's passive DNS) store resolutions observed by sensors over years; they answer "what has ever pointed at this IP?" and "what did this hostname resolve to in 2023?" without you querying the target's name servers. Reverse lookups on a company's mail or web IP space frequently surface forgotten subsidiaries and staging systems.</p>
<h4>Certificate transparency: the richest passive source</h4>
<p>Since 2018 Chrome has required every publicly trusted TLS certificate to be logged in public <strong>certificate transparency</strong> logs, which makes CT the single best passive host list. Querying crt.sh for <code>%.example.com</code> returns every certificate ever issued for a subdomain, including internal-sounding names (vpn, jenkins, grafana-old) that administrators assumed were obscure. Wildcards hide names, but most organizations issue per-host certificates through automation, so the log grows daily. The concept behind <em>subdomain enumeration</em> is to union these passive sources (CT, passive DNS, search engines, web archives, Shodan and Censys host records) and only then, if authorized, resolve and probe them; tools such as amass and subfinder have passive modes that do exactly this. Brute-forcing names against the target's DNS is active and belongs in a Domain 8 engagement.</p>
<pre># passive host discovery from certificate transparency (your own domain)
curl -s "https://crt.sh/?q=%25.example.com&amp;output=json" | jq -r '.[].name_value' | sort -u
# resolve the candidates you are authorized to assess, then compare with the asset inventory
while read h; do echo "$h $(dig +short "$h" | head -1)"; done &lt; hosts.txt</pre>
<h4>Internet-wide scanners</h4>
<p>Shodan and Censys continuously scan the entire IPv4 space and index banners, certificates, screenshots and known vulnerabilities, which means searching them is passive: the scanning already happened. Searching your own organization (<code>org:"Example Corp"</code>, <code>ssl.cert.subject.cn:example.com</code>, your netblocks) shows what the world sees: an RDP port on a forgotten cloud VM, a management interface with a default banner, a printer, an industrial controller, an expired certificate. Shodan Monitor and Censys ASM exist precisely so defenders watch their own ranges continuously. Reading the results needs Domain 2: a port is a service, a banner is a version, a certificate's subject alternative names are more hostnames.</p>
<h4>Code, cloud storage and credentials</h4>
<p>Developers leak. GitHub code search for a company domain beside words like <em>password</em>, <em>apikey</em> or <em>BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY</em> finds secrets committed to public repositories; GitHub's own secret scanning and tools such as gitleaks or truffleHog run against repositories you control to find them first. Cloud object storage is the second classic: an S3 bucket, Azure blob container or GCS bucket with public listing exposes backups and customer files, and bucket names are guessable from brand names; AWS has blocked public access by default on new buckets since April 2023, but older buckets and other providers still produce headlines (Accenture's and Verizon's 2017 exposures through third parties remain the textbook cases). Then credentials: Have I Been Pwned offers a free domain search to verified domain owners that reports which corporate addresses appear in known breaches; the defender's response is forced resets and MFA, never downloading the dumps. Finally, email formats (first.last, flast) are inferred from public patterns to judge how easily an attacker could spray or spoof, not to enumerate people.</p>
<h4>Job postings and the tech-stack leak</h4>
<p>A posting that asks for "experience with Fortinet FortiGate, Okta, Splunk and Citrix NetScaler" has published the security stack, and a request for "Windows Server 2012 R2 migration" has published the end-of-life systems. Vendor case studies, press releases, conference talks and public GitHub organizations do the same. Collect it as part of the map and recommend that recruiters describe skills generically.</p>
<h4>From the map to action</h4>
<table><tr><th>Finding</th><th>Authorized pentest use (Domain 8)</th><th>Blue-team reduction (Domain 21)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Unknown subdomains from CT</td><td>Candidates for the scoped recon and scanning phases</td><td>Reconcile with the asset inventory; decommission or bring under monitoring</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exposed management service</td><td>In-scope target for vulnerability analysis</td><td>Firewall it, put it behind VPN or zero trust, patch</td></tr>
<tr><td>Public repository secret</td><td>Report immediately; never use</td><td>Rotate the key, add secret scanning and pre-commit hooks</td></tr>
<tr><td>Corporate emails in breaches</td><td>Context for password-spray risk, within rules of engagement</td><td>Force resets, phishing-resistant MFA, breach-notification monitoring</td></tr>
<tr><td>Job posting stack leak</td><td>Shapes the threat scenario</td><td>Recruiting guidance; assume the stack is known</td></tr></table>
<p>The output is an attack-surface map: a table of assets with source, first-seen date, owner, and a risk note, delivered with the collection log so anyone can reproduce it. In an authorized penetration test this map is the reconnaissance phase and defines what the active phases may touch. For a defender it becomes the backlog for attack-surface reduction and a metric: the count of unexpected internet-facing assets per quarter, driven toward zero.</p>
<p><em>Exam mapping.</em> GOSI's "Investigating Businesses" and "Network Data and Infrastructure Analysis" objectives are this module: public and breach data about an organization, domains, IP addresses, certificates and networks, collected passively and reported with a reduction plan. CTIA tests the same material as the collection stage against an organization's threat landscape.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>A map with numbers (fictional company, two known domains).</strong> Company filings add a third brand domain from a 2021 acquisition. crt.sh returns 134 certificate entries across the three domains; deduplication leaves 41 unique hostnames. Passive DNS adds 7 more from historical records. Of the 48 candidates, 29 resolve today; 19 are dead names still in the logs. Shodan shows 6 of the 29 with services beyond 80/443: two with RDP (3389), one with an exposed Grafana login, one Fortinet VPN portal, one printer and one host whose certificate expired in 2024. GitHub search finds one repository from a former contractor containing an API key for a mapping service (low privilege, still a finding). Have I Been Pwned's domain search reports 212 corporate addresses in 14 breaches, 3 of them from 2026. Deliverable: 48 rows, 6 flagged for immediate action, a recommendation to decommission the 19 dead names from DNS and certificates, and a 90-day plan to re-run the map and measure the count of unexpected assets falling from 6 toward 0. Nobody's name appears in the report.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"Searching Shodan for a company is hacking.\" Shodan already scanned the internet; reading its index is passive. Scanning the hosts yourself without authorization is not.","\"Redacted WHOIS means the domain is a dead end.\" Registrar, dates, name servers, historical records, passive DNS and certificate logs still tie domains together.","\"Checking breach exposure means downloading the breach.\" Possessing stolen credentials is the attacker's business; defenders use Have I Been Pwned's domain search and force resets."],
problems:[
{q:"Write a scoping statement for an attack-surface assessment that keeps it legal and people-free.", a:"Targets: domains and IP ranges listed in writing by the asset owner (or in a public bounty scope). Methods: passive sources only (CT, passive DNS, Shodan/Censys, public code search, HIBP domain search); no scanning, no login attempts, no contact. Exclusions: employees' personal accounts and any profiling of individuals. Output: asset table with sources and a reduction plan."},
{q:"crt.sh returns 300 entries for your domain but only 60 unique names, of which 22 resolve. Explain the three numbers and what to do with the 38 that do not resolve.", a:"Certificates are reissued (renewals), so entries exceed names; many names are retired but still logged. Dead names are still intelligence: remove stale DNS, revoke or let certificates lapse, and check whether any now points at a third-party service that could be taken over (dangling records)."},
{q:"A Censys result shows a host with TLS certificate SANs for five other hostnames not in your inventory. What does that tell you and what is the next passive step?", a:"The host serves (or served) those names; they are likely yours or a shared provider's. Check CT and passive DNS for each, compare with the inventory, and flag them as unknown assets for the owner to claim or retire."},
{q:"Your scan of your own GitHub organization finds a committed AWS key from 2023 that was later removed in a new commit. Is the risk gone?", a:"No: history retains it and anyone may have copied it. Rotate the key, audit CloudTrail for use, add secret scanning and pre-commit hooks, and consider the repository history compromised."},
{q:"Compute the exposure metric: Q1 map found 9 unexpected internet-facing assets across 300 known; Q2 found 4 across 310. Report the change the way an executive would read it.", a:"Unexpected assets fell from 3.0% (9/300) to 1.3% (4/310), a 56% reduction in a quarter; name the four remaining and the date each will be fixed."}
],
checks:[{q:"What does crt.sh search?",a:"Public certificate transparency logs of issued TLS certificates."},{q:"Passive or active: amass in passive mode; nmap -sV?",a:"Passive; active (authorization required)."},{q:"Date ICANN ended the WHOIS port-43 obligation for gTLDs?",a:"January 28, 2025 (RDAP replaced it)."}],
explain:"Walk ADA through building an attack-surface map of a fictional company from passive sources only, saying for each source why it is passive, and finish with how a pentester and a defender each use the result."},

{id:"p32m4", title:"Image, Geolocation and Verification: Bellingcat Methods for Journalism and Research", minutes:55,
outcomes:["Run a reverse image search across engines and read what each is good at.","Extract and interpret EXIF metadata and know when it is missing or forged.","Geolocate a scene from landmarks, signage, infrastructure and terrain, and chronolocate it from shadows with SunCalc arithmetic.","Verify video with keyframes, upload metadata and corroboration, and judge synthetic media by provenance.","Apply all of it only to public events, your own media and practice sets."],
body:`<p>The question: given a photograph or a video that claims to show something, how do you establish where and when it was really taken, and whether it is real at all? This is the craft that let Bellingcat and others reconstruct the downing of Malaysia Airlines flight MH17 in 2014 from social-media posts of a Buk launcher's route, and that let BBC Africa Eye identify where and roughly when soldiers filmed in Cameroon committed killings in 2015 using a mountain ridge, building outlines, a footpath and the sun. It is journalism and human-rights work first, a security skill second (verifying a leaked screenshot, checking a vendor's "breach" claim). One boundary applies before any technique: you geolocate <em>events and places in the public interest, your own media and practice sets</em>. You never apply it to a private individual's home, routine or whereabouts. Facial search against private people is excluded for the same reason.</p>
<h4>Reverse image search</h4>
<p><strong>Reverse image search</strong> asks engines where else an image, or a visually similar one, appears. Each engine has a character: Google Lens is strongest at objects, landmarks and text; Bing Visual Search lets you crop to a region; Yandex is remarkably good at landscapes and buildings; TinEye finds the earliest indexed copy, which answers the most common verification question, "is this old footage recycled for a new event?" Search the whole frame, then crops of distinctive features (a sign, a tower, a vehicle livery), and record the earliest dated appearance as evidence.</p>
<h4>EXIF and other metadata</h4>
<p><strong>EXIF</strong> is the metadata block cameras write into JPEG and RAW files: make and model, lens, exposure, software, the original capture timestamp, and, if the device had location enabled, GPS latitude, longitude and altitude. Phil Harvey's <code>exiftool</code> reads all of it. Two cautions: most major social platforms strip EXIF on upload, so its absence means little; and every field is trivially editable, so a GPS tag is a lead to corroborate, never a conclusion. Video files carry similar metadata (creation time, encoder), and documents carry author and revision data, which is why your own OPSEC includes stripping metadata before you publish anything (<code>exiftool -all= photo.jpg</code>).</p>
<pre>exiftool -DateTimeOriginal -Make -Model -GPSLatitude -GPSLongitude -Software IMG_2041.jpg
Date/Time Original : 2026:03:14 16:42:07
Make               : Apple          Model : iPhone 15
GPS Latitude       : 29 deg 31' 48.10" N      GPS Longitude : 98 deg 42' 36.40" W
Software           : 18.3.1</pre>
<h4>Geolocation: reading the scene</h4>
<p><strong>Geolocation</strong> is the disciplined extraction of every locational clue and its confirmation against maps and imagery. Work from coarse to fine. Country and region: language and script on signs, road-marking colors, license-plate shape and color (country level only), traffic side, utility-pole construction, bollards, architecture, vegetation and climate. District: street layout, a distinctive roofline, a church or minaret, a stadium, water, a hill profile. Exact spot: match the arrangement of features to satellite imagery (Google Earth Pro, with historical imagery to confirm the date range of a building), street-level imagery (Street View, Mapillary, KartaView) and OpenStreetMap queries via Overpass for "a school within 200 m of a railway and a water tower," which Bellingcat's OpenStreetMap search tool wraps for you. Mountains are matched with PeakVisor's silhouettes. Write down each clue, what it rules in and rules out, and the confidence it adds, exactly as in ACH: a location is confirmed when multiple independent features align and no feature contradicts it.</p>
<h4>Chronolocation: the sun as a clock</h4>
<p><strong>Chronolocation</strong> estimates <em>when</em>. Shadows are the primary instrument. On a known date and place, SunCalc (suncalc.org) gives the sun's azimuth and elevation at any time; conversely, a measured shadow constrains the time or the date. The arithmetic: for an object of height h casting a shadow of length L on flat ground, the solar elevation e satisfies tan(e) = h / L. A 2.0 m signpost with a 2.7 m shadow gives e = arctan(2.0 / 2.7) ≈ 36.5°. Shadow direction gives the azimuth (shadows point away from the sun). In San Antonio on March 14, 2026, an elevation near 36° with the shadow pointing east-northeast matches late afternoon, roughly 16:30–16:50 local, consistent with the EXIF stamp above; the morning solution (shadow pointing west) is excluded by the direction. Bellingcat's Shadow Finder tool (2024) runs the inverse problem across the globe: given a date, time and shadow ratio, it draws the band of latitudes and longitudes where that shadow is possible. Other clocks: seasonal vegetation, snow, historical weather records, shop opening hours, construction progress visible in dated satellite imagery, and the moon's phase.</p>
<h4>Video verification and synthetic media</h4>
<p>Video is verified frame by frame. The InVID-WeVerify browser plugin extracts keyframes for reverse search, magnifies regions and reads metadata; Amnesty International's YouTube DataViewer exposes exact upload times and thumbnails; audio (language, accents, sirens, calls to prayer) and weather are corroborating clocks. The commonest fake is not synthetic at all but <em>recycled</em>: real footage from another place or year. Synthetic media, the <strong>deepfake</strong>, needs a different posture. Visual tells still exist (inconsistent lighting and reflections, warped hands and teeth, mismatched earrings, garbled text, boundary blending, lip-sync drift) but detectors fail against new generators, so the durable method is <em>provenance plus corroboration</em>: Content Credentials under the C2PA standard cryptographically bind capture and edit history to a file when the camera or tool supports it; absent that, you ask where the file first appeared, who else recorded the event, and whether independent sources agree. The 2024 Arup case, in which an employee wired about HK$200 million (roughly US$25 million) after a video call populated by deepfaked colleagues, shows why verification is a corporate control as much as a journalistic one (Module 6).</p>
<h4>Practice that stays ethical</h4>
<p>Train on sanctioned sets: Sofia Santos's free OSINT exercises at gralhix.com, OSINT Dojo's ranked challenges, Bellingcat's published how-tos and challenge write-ups, GeoGuessr for raw scene-reading speed, and your own photographs with the EXIF as the answer key. Record each solve as a case file: clues, sources, confidence, and the one feature that would have changed your answer.</p>
<p><em>Exam mapping.</em> GOSI's "Searching, Collecting, and Processing Data" objective covers metadata and multimedia handling, and SEC497's imagery and geolocation labs come from this material; the journalism standards (Berkeley Protocol, sourcing, verification before publication) are the same standards ICD 203 asks of an analyst.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>One photograph, start to finish.</strong> A news tip shows a flooded underpass "in San Antonio this morning." EXIF is stripped (shared through a messaging app), so the file itself is silent. TinEye finds no earlier copy; Google Lens matches the mural visible on the retaining wall to a 2023 article naming the street. Street View confirms the mural, the lane count and a distinctive green pedestrian bridge: location confirmed by three independent features, none contradicting. For time: a traffic-sign post about 2.4 m tall casts a 1.6 m shadow pointing a little north of west, so the elevation is arctan(2.4/1.6) ≈ 56° and the sun stands a little south of east; SunCalc for that spot on the claimed late-May date gives 56° at about 11:05 local, a morning solution, consistent with "this morning." Historical weather for the date records 40 mm of rain overnight. Judgment: almost certain (high confidence) that the image shows the named underpass; very likely taken on the claimed morning. Deliverable: the annotated image, the feature table, the SunCalc screenshot and the weather record, with hashes and timestamps.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"No EXIF means the photo was tampered with.\" Most platforms strip metadata routinely; absence is uninformative. Presence is a lead, not proof, because every field can be edited.","\"Shadows tell you the exact time.\" They constrain elevation and azimuth; with a known date and place they give a narrow window, and with unknown date they give a band of possibilities. Combine with other clocks.","\"A deepfake detector settles it.\" Detectors lag generators; provenance (C2PA Content Credentials, first appearance) and independent corroboration are the durable tests."],
problems:[
{q:"A 1.8 m person casts a 1.8 m shadow. Compute the solar elevation and say what it implies about time of day near the equinox in Texas.", a:"tan(e) = 1.8/1.8 = 1, so e = 45°. Near the equinox at about 29.5° N the sun reaches roughly 60° at solar noon, so 45° occurs twice: mid-morning and mid-afternoon; shadow direction (west of north in the morning, east of north in the afternoon) decides which."},
{q:"You find the same video on TinEye dated two years earlier in another country. Write the judgment sentence.", a:"We assess it is almost certain (high confidence) that the video is recycled: an identical copy was indexed on [date] attributed to [place], predating the claimed event; the current claim is false."},
{q:"List five country-level clues in a street scene and one trap in each.", a:"Language/script (border regions and diaspora shops), plate shape/color (foreign vehicles), driving side (one-way streets), road-marking colors (temporary works), utility poles (imported standards); confirm with a second independent class of clue."},
{q:"A photo's EXIF GPS places it at your own address and the file is about to be posted. What do you run and why?", a:"exiftool -all= photo.jpg (or export without metadata) to strip location and device data; publishing GPS tags is the footprint leak you audited in Module 2."},
{q:"Design a verification checklist for a video a vendor sends as 'proof' of a breach of your company, without touching any attacker infrastructure.", a:"Keyframes to reverse search; upload metadata and first appearance; internal corroboration (does the UI, hostname or data in the frames match real systems?); audio and language; metadata of the file; ask for a hash of the original; grade the vendor as a source; state confidence before anyone acts."}
],
checks:[{q:"Which engine is best for finding the earliest copy of an image?",a:"TinEye."},{q:"Formula linking shadow length, object height and solar elevation?",a:"tan(elevation) = height / shadow length."},{q:"Standard behind Content Credentials?",a:"C2PA."}],
explain:"Teach ADA to verify one photograph: reverse search, EXIF, scene clues from coarse to fine, the shadow arithmetic with SunCalc, and the sentence that states location and time with confidence. State the ethical boundary first."},
{id:"p32m5", title:"Threat Intelligence in Practice: IOCs, TTPs, ATT&CK, the Diamond Model, STIX/TAXII and MISP", minutes:60,
outcomes:["Separate indicators of compromise from tactics, techniques and procedures and place both on the Pyramid of Pain.","Map a public incident report to MITRE ATT&CK technique IDs and draw its Diamond Model.","Read and write a STIX 2.1 bundle and explain what TAXII adds.","Stand up MISP in Docker, load a feed, and judge feed quality with arithmetic.","Profile a threat actor from public reporting and know where dark-web monitoring (Domain 47) fits."],
body:`<p>The question: what should a defender actually consume from the flood of "threat intel," and in what form, so that it changes a detection or a decision? The answer begins with a distinction. An <strong>IOC</strong>, indicator of compromise, is an observable fact about a specific attack: a file hash, an IP address, a domain, a mutex name, a registry key. It is cheap to collect, cheap to match and cheap for the adversary to change. A <strong>TTP</strong>, tactics, techniques and procedures, describes <em>how</em> the adversary operates: phishing with an ISO attachment that mounts and runs a loader, then dumping credentials from LSASS, then moving laterally over SMB with a valid account. Change the hash and the IOC dies; the TTP survives until the adversary retrains.</p>
<h4>The Pyramid of Pain</h4>
<p>David Bianco's <strong>Pyramid of Pain</strong> (2013) ranks indicator types by how much it costs the adversary when you detect them. From the bottom: hash values (trivial to change: recompile), IP addresses (easy: new VPS), domain names (simple: register another), network and host artifacts (annoying: user-agent strings, file paths, registry keys), tools (challenging: the attacker must rebuild or buy new tooling), and at the apex TTPs (tough: changing behavior means retraining the operation). A program that only blocks hashes lives at the bottom of the pyramid forever. ATT&CK exists so teams can work at the top.</p>
<h4>Kill chain and MITRE ATT&CK</h4>
<p>Lockheed Martin's Cyber Kill Chain (2011) gave the first common vocabulary: reconnaissance, weaponization, delivery, exploitation, installation, command and control, actions on objectives. <strong>MITRE ATT&CK</strong> replaced that linear story with a matrix built from public incident reporting. Enterprise ATT&CK v20.0 (released October 13, 2026) holds 15 tactics (the adversary's goals, Reconnaissance through Impact), 227 techniques and 479 sub-techniques (how each goal is achieved), with procedure examples per group and software, mitigations, and since v18 (October 2025) Detection Strategies with concrete Analytics; v20 added AI-focused techniques such as prompt injection. Mapping a report means tagging each described behavior: "the actor sent a spearphishing link" is T1566.002; "logged in with stolen VPN credentials" is T1078 Valid Accounts; "ran a PowerShell downloader" is T1059.001; "encrypted the file servers" is T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact. The ATT&CK Navigator lays those IDs on the matrix as a heat map; overlaying your detection coverage shows the gaps a red team should test (Domain 8) and a SOC should hunt (Domain 21).</p>
<h4>The Diamond Model</h4>
<p>Caltagirone, Pendergast and Betz published <strong>The Diamond Model of Intrusion Analysis</strong> in 2013 as a way to reason about any intrusion event. Four core features sit at the corners: <em>adversary</em> (who, with intent and resources), <em>capability</em> (malware, exploits, tools), <em>infrastructure</em> (domains, servers, accounts the capability uses) and <em>victim</em> (the organization and its assets). The edges are the analyst's pivots: the victim's logs reveal infrastructure, infrastructure reveals capability, capability hints at adversary. Meta-features add timestamp, phase, result, direction, methodology and resources. Events chain into <em>activity threads</em> (one adversary against one victim, ordered by phase) and threads cluster into <em>activity groups</em> by shared features, which is how a nameless intrusion becomes a tracked actor. Its first axiom is the field's creed: for every intrusion event there is an adversary taking a step toward a goal.</p>
<h4>STIX 2.1 and TAXII 2.1</h4>
<p>Intelligence is shared in structured form so machines can act on it. <strong>STIX</strong> 2.1 (OASIS, 2021) is a JSON language with 18 domain objects (indicator, malware, attack-pattern, threat-actor, intrusion-set, campaign, infrastructure, vulnerability, report and others), relationship and sighting objects that connect them, and cyber-observable objects (file, ipv4-addr, domain-name, url). An indicator carries a <em>pattern</em> such as <code>[file:hashes.'SHA-256' = '…']</code> and a validity window. <strong>TAXII</strong> 2.1 is the HTTPS transport: a server exposes API roots and collections, clients poll <code>GET /{api-root}/collections/{id}/objects/</code> with an <code>added_after</code> filter, so feeds update incrementally over TLS with authentication. A bundle is the JSON envelope:</p>
<pre>{ "type": "bundle", "id": "bundle--3f1d…", "objects": [
  { "type": "indicator", "spec_version": "2.1", "id": "indicator--a4b2…",
    "created": "2026-10-20T14:02:00.000Z", "modified": "2026-10-20T14:02:00.000Z",
    "name": "Loader C2 domain", "pattern_type": "stix",
    "pattern": "[domain-name:value = 'update-check[.]example']",
    "valid_from": "2026-10-20T00:00:00Z", "labels": ["malicious-activity"] },
  { "type": "relationship", "spec_version": "2.1", "id": "relationship--9c0e…",
    "relationship_type": "indicates", "source_ref": "indicator--a4b2…", "target_ref": "malware--77de…" } ] }</pre>
<h4>Platforms: MISP and OpenCTI</h4>
<p><strong>MISP</strong>, the open source threat-sharing platform maintained by CIRCL and the MISP Project (AGPL; release 2.5.50 in October 2026, with a 3.0 early-access branch), stores <em>events</em> made of <em>attributes</em> and objects, tags them with taxonomies (TLP, the IC estimative-language scale), links them to <em>galaxies</em> (ATT&CK techniques, threat actors with their vendor aliases, ransomware families), pulls default feeds, correlates attributes across events, exports STIX, OpenIOC and IDS rules, and synchronizes with other instances through sharing groups. The official <code>misp-docker</code> compose files bring it up in minutes; PyMISP automates it over the REST API. OpenCTI (Filigran, Apache-2.0, version 6.x) is the knowledge-graph alternative: native STIX 2.1 data model, ingestion through connectors (MISP, ATT&CK, abuse.ch, vendor feeds), running on Elasticsearch or OpenSearch, Redis, RabbitMQ and MinIO under Docker Compose. Many teams run both: MISP to share indicators, OpenCTI as the analyst's knowledge base.</p>
<h4>Feeds and their quality</h4>
<p>Free feeds are excellent and dangerous. abuse.ch (URLhaus, ThreatFox, MalwareBazaar, Feodo Tracker), CISA's advisories and the AIS program, vendor blogs and MISP's default feed list deliver tens of thousands of IOCs a day. Quality is measured, not assumed: timeliness (age at delivery), uniqueness (overlap with feeds you already have), context (is a technique and actor attached?), and false-positive rate. The arithmetic decides: a feed of 50,000 indicators per day with a 2% false-positive rate matched against your logs produces about 1,000 false alerts daily, more than a small SOC's total capacity; the same feed filtered to the 500 indicators relevant to your PIRs produces about 10. Relevance filtering by requirement is the whole point of Module 1.</p>
<h4>Threat actor profiling</h4>
<p>A <em>threat actor</em> profile answers: who, why, how, against whom, and what changed. Three motivational classes, with public examples: nation-state (APT29, the SVR-attributed group behind the 2020 SolarWinds supply-chain campaign; APT28 of the GRU; Volt Typhoon, the PRC-attributed group pre-positioning in U.S. critical infrastructure), criminal (LockBit, disrupted by Operation Cronos in February 2024 but not extinguished; Scattered Spider, the English-speaking crew behind the 2023 MGM Resorts help-desk vishing and 2025 retail intrusions; North Korea's Lazarus Group, state-run yet financially motivated, credited with the February 2025 Bybit theft of about 1.5 billion dollars in crypto assets), and hacktivist (Killnet and allied pro-Russian collectives running DDoS campaigns). Vendor naming is a mess by design: Microsoft calls APT29 Midnight Blizzard, CrowdStrike says Cozy Bear, Mandiant says APT29; MISP's threat-actor galaxy maps the aliases. Profile from public reporting only, grade each source with the Admiralty code, express attribution with estimative language (attribution is a judgment, never a fact), and end with the ATT&CK techniques the actor favors, because that is what your detections consume. Dark-web monitoring (leak sites, forums, markets) feeds these profiles and has its own rules of engagement; Domain 47 covers it, and nothing here requires visiting a criminal forum.</p>
<p><em>Exam mapping.</em> This module is the core of EC-Council CTIA (threat actors, kill chain, Diamond Model, ATT&CK, feeds, STIX/TAXII, source grading, sharing) and the frameworks every SOC certification assumes (Domain 21). GOSI does not test platforms, but SEC497's capstone is a collaborative threat assessment that uses exactly this vocabulary.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>From vendor report to detection in one sitting.</strong> A public report describes a campaign: phishing email with an ISO attachment; the ISO contains a shortcut that runs a PowerShell loader; the loader beacons to three domains; credentials dumped with a renamed procdump; lateral movement via SMB admin shares; ransomware deployed by PsExec. Mapping: T1566.001 (spearphishing attachment), T1204.002 (user execution: malicious file), T1059.001 (PowerShell), T1071.001 (web protocols for C2), T1003.001 (LSASS memory), T1021.002 (SMB/admin shares), T1570 (lateral tool transfer), T1486 (data encrypted for impact). Diamond: adversary = tracked criminal group (moderate confidence, based on tooling overlap in two vendor reports graded B2); capability = the loader and ransomware; infrastructure = three domains and two IPs; victim = your sector. In MISP you create one event with 14 attributes (3 domains, 2 IPs, 6 hashes, 3 file names), tag it tlp:amber and the eight ATT&CK galaxy clusters, and push it to the SIEM. The IOCs expire in weeks; the eight techniques become hunt hypotheses (ISO mounts by users, PowerShell spawned from Explorer, procdump renamed) that keep working after the domains change. That is the Pyramid of Pain in practice.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"More IOC feeds means better defense.\" Unfiltered feeds mostly generate false positives; value comes from relevance to your requirements and from TTP-level knowledge, not volume.","\"ATT&CK is a checklist of attacks to block.\" It is a knowledge base of observed behaviors; use it to map reports, measure detection coverage and design hunts and red-team scenarios.","\"Attribution is settled when a vendor names a group.\" Attribution is an analytic judgment with confidence; vendors use different names for overlapping clusters and sometimes disagree."],
problems:[
{q:"Place these on the Pyramid of Pain and justify: a SHA-256 of a loader; the attacker's habit of staging tools in C:\\PerfLogs; use of a renamed procdump; the C2 IP; the technique of ISO-based phishing.", a:"Hash: bottom (recompile). C2 IP: low (new VPS). Staging path: host artifact (annoying). Renamed procdump: tool level (challenging; they must change tooling). ISO phishing with LNK execution: TTP (tough; a workflow change)."},
{q:"A feed delivers 80,000 IOCs/day at a 1.5% false-positive rate; your SOC can triage 150 alerts/day. Compute the problem and propose the fix.", a:"80,000 × 0.015 = 1,200 false alerts/day, eight times capacity. Filter to indicators matching your PIRs and sector (say 2,000/day → 30 false alerts), raise confidence thresholds, and use the feed for enrichment rather than alerting."},
{q:"Write a STIX 2.1 relationship that says an intrusion set uses a malware family, naming the three required reference fields.", a:"A relationship object with relationship_type 'uses', source_ref = intrusion-set--…, target_ref = malware--…, plus type, spec_version, id, created and modified."},
{q:"Draw the Diamond for a BEC incident: a lookalike domain emailed the finance team from a hosted mailbox and redirected a vendor payment.", a:"Adversary: BEC crew (unknown, low confidence). Capability: lookalike domain plus pretext email (no malware). Infrastructure: the registered domain, its mail provider, the receiving bank account. Victim: finance staff and the vendor-payment process. Meta: phase = action on objectives; result = success; methodology = social engineering."},
{q:"Why does a MISP galaxy entry for a threat actor list many names, and what does that imply for your profile?", a:"Each vendor clusters activity independently and names it; overlaps are partial. Your profile must state which vendor's cluster you mean, list aliases, grade each source, and express attribution with estimative language."}
],
checks:[{q:"Enterprise ATT&CK v20.0 counts?",a:"15 tactics, 227 techniques, 479 sub-techniques (October 13, 2026)."},{q:"Four core features of the Diamond Model?",a:"Adversary, capability, infrastructure, victim."},{q:"What does TAXII add to STIX?",a:"The HTTPS transport: API roots, collections, incremental polling with authentication."}],
explain:"Explain to ADA why a hash is weak intelligence and a technique is strong, using the Pyramid of Pain; then map a short incident narrative to five ATT&CK IDs and a Diamond, and describe how the result lands in MISP."},

{id:"p32m6", title:"Social Engineering Defense: Psychology, Vectors, BEC Case Studies and Programs That Work", minutes:55,
outcomes:["Explain the persuasion principles and emotional triggers attackers exploit, as recognition cues.","Describe every major social-engineering vector by channel and tell-tale signs, defensively.","Analyze business email compromise case studies and extract the control that would have stopped each.","Design an awareness program and an authorized phishing simulation with consent, rules and the right metrics.","Write verification protocols and physical-security basics that remove the attacker's leverage."],
body:`<p>The question: if most breaches begin with a person being persuaded rather than a system being exploited, what does a defense of people look like? Verizon's 2025 Data Breach Investigations Report again put the human element in about 60% of breaches, and the FBI's Internet Crime Complaint Center counted 1,008,597 complaints and about 20.9 billion dollars in reported losses for 2025, with <em>business email compromise</em> alone at roughly 3.05 billion dollars. The attacker's method is <strong>social engineering</strong>: manipulating people into actions or disclosures that defeat controls. This module teaches it for recognition and defense only. There are no scripts, pretext templates or lure designs here, and none are needed to defend.</p>
<h4>The psychology</h4>
<p>Robert Cialdini's principles of influence, from <em>Influence</em> (1984) plus the seventh added in 2016, are the attacker's toolkit and your recognition cues: <em>reciprocity</em> (a favor creates a debt), <em>commitment and consistency</em> (small yes leads to big yes), <em>social proof</em> ("everyone in finance has already done this"), <em>authority</em> (the CEO, the auditor, IT, the police), <em>liking</em> (rapport built from interests harvested from public profiles, the OSINT exposure you audited in Module 2), <em>scarcity</em> (last chance), and <em>unity</em> (we are on the same team). People are the human attack surface, and the same passive mapping that finds a forgotten server finds the organizational chart a pretext needs. Layered on top are the two accelerants that appear in almost every real attack: <em>urgency</em> and <em>fear</em>. Chris Hadnagy calls the result an amygdala hijack: a strong emotion narrows attention and suppresses the deliberate checking that would catch the lie. The defensive lesson is mechanical: any request that combines authority, urgency and a deviation from process is, by definition, suspicious, however plausible its story.</p>
<h4>The vectors, for recognition</h4>
<table><tr><th>Vector</th><th>Channel</th><th>Recognition cues</th><th>Primary defense</th></tr>
<tr><td>Phishing / spear phishing / whaling</td><td>Email, targeted or at executives</td><td>Lookalike domain, mismatched reply-to, urgency, credential or payment ask, unexpected attachment or QR code</td><td>DMARC enforcement, link isolation, phishing-resistant MFA, report button</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vishing</td><td>Phone, increasingly with cloned voices</td><td>Caller asserts authority, refuses callback, demands a code or password reset</td><td>Callback on a known number; help-desk identity verification</td></tr>
<tr><td>Smishing</td><td>SMS and messaging apps</td><td>Shortened links, delivery or payroll pretexts, "verify your account"</td><td>Never act from a text; use the official app</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pretexting</td><td>Any channel</td><td>A detailed, plausible story that explains why normal process should be skipped</td><td>Process has no urgency exception</td></tr>
<tr><td>Baiting</td><td>USB drops, fake downloads, free tools</td><td>Unsolicited media; too-good software</td><td>USB policy, application control</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tailgating / piggybacking</td><td>Physical</td><td>Hands full, no badge, "forgot my card," delivery uniform</td><td>Badge-in culture, mantraps, escort policy</td></tr>
<tr><td>MFA fatigue, help-desk reset</td><td>Push prompts, support calls</td><td>Repeated prompts you did not start; a "user" asking support to re-enroll MFA</td><td>Number matching or FIDO2; strict re-enrollment verification</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deepfake video or voice</td><td>Video calls, voicemail</td><td>Unusual payment request in an unusual meeting</td><td>Out-of-band confirmation, dual authorization</td></tr></table>
<p><strong>Pretexting</strong> deserves its own sentence because it is the engine inside the others: a fabricated scenario that makes a harmful action feel like the normal thing to do. The scenario is always richer than it needs to be, because detail buys trust. <strong>Vishing</strong> is pretexting by voice, and since 2023 commercial voice cloning has made "it sounded exactly like her" worthless as verification. Smishing is the same on SMS, where the small screen hides the real link.</p>
<h4>Business email compromise: the case file</h4>
<p><strong>Business email compromise</strong> (BEC) is the professional's social engineering: no malware, just a convincing instruction to move money or data. FACC, the Austrian aerospace supplier, wired about 50 million euros in 2016 on emails impersonating its CEO; the CEO and CFO were dismissed. Ubiquiti lost 46.7 million dollars in 2015 to impersonated executives and vendors. Between 2013 and 2015 Evaldas Rimasauskas invoiced Facebook and Google more than 100 million dollars by posing as a real hardware supplier; both paid, and he was sentenced to five years in 2019. Toyota Boshoku's European subsidiary lost about 37 million dollars in 2019 to a fake vendor banking change. In January 2024 an Arup employee in Hong Kong transferred about HK$200 million after a video call in which the "CFO" and colleagues were deepfakes. The pattern never varies: authority plus urgency plus a change to payment details, outside the normal channel, with no independent verification. The control is also constant: a payment-change or wire request is confirmed by calling a number already on file, never one in the message, and any transfer above a threshold needs two approvers. The 2023 MGM Resorts intrusion, opened by a ten-minute vishing call to the help desk by Scattered Spider impersonating an employee, teaches IT the same lesson: identity re-enrollment needs verification a stranger with public information cannot pass. Cloudflare's 2022 experience is the counterexample: hit by the same smishing campaign that compromised Twilio, its staff entered credentials on the fake page, but FIDO2 security keys bound to the real origin made the stolen credentials useless.</p>
<h4>Awareness programs that work</h4>
<p>NIST SP 800-50 Revision 1 (2024), <em>Building a Cybersecurity and Privacy Learning Program</em>, frames awareness as a program with roles, not an annual video. What the evidence supports: short, frequent, role-based content (finance gets BEC, the help desk gets identity verification, engineers get token and repository hygiene); teaching the <em>cues</em> (authority, urgency, process deviation) rather than memorizing lures; a one-click report button with fast, kind feedback; zero blame for being fooled, because punishment drives silence; and leadership that models the behavior. Measure what matters. Click rate falls with easy lures and rises with hard ones. Report rate and time-to-first-report are the operational numbers: an organization where 40% of recipients report a real phish within ten minutes is defended whatever its click rate, because the SOC pulls the message before most people open it. Vendor benchmarks consistently show untrained populations clicking about a third of simulated phish and well-run programs reaching single digits within a year.</p>
<h4>Authorized phishing simulations</h4>
<p>A simulation is an authorized test with rules, not a prank. Requirements: written authorization from an executive sponsor with HR and legal signed on; a policy, acknowledged by staff, stating that simulations occur (consent at the program level); defined scope, schedule and exclusions; lures that teach rather than humiliate (GoDaddy's December 2020 "holiday bonus" test and Tribune Publishing's bonus lure the same year became public-relations disasters and taught contempt); no capture of real passwords (the landing page records the attempt, not the secret); retention limits for results; just-in-time training on click; aggregate reporting, never a wall of shame. GoPhish is the open source platform; commercial platforms add content libraries and LMS integration. Metrics per campaign: delivered, opened, clicked, submitted, reported, median time-to-report, trended by department and lure difficulty. Pair simulations with structural fixes, because training alone plateaus: phishing-resistant MFA (FIDO2 passkeys or security keys), DMARC at p=reject (Domain 21), link isolation and payment-process controls.</p>
<h4>Verification protocols and physical basics</h4>
<p>Write the protocols down so people can follow them under pressure: callback to a number on file for any payment, banking or credential change; dual authorization above a threshold; help-desk identity checks using information an outsider cannot assemble from public sources, with manager confirmation for MFA re-enrollment; a stated rule that urgency never waives a step; and for voice clones at home, a family code word. Physical security follows the same logic: badge every door with no "hold it for me," visitor escort and sign-in, clean desks and locked screens, shredding instead of trash, and a culture where challenging a stranger is normal and thanked.</p>
<p><em>Exam mapping.</em> Social engineering recognition and controls appear in Security+ and CySA+ (Domain 7 and Domain 21), in CTIA's threat-landscape domain, and in GOSI's "Investigating People" objective reframed defensively: understanding how an organization's public exposure enables pretexting is what justifies the verification protocols above.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>Designing a program with numbers.</strong> A 600-person contractor runs a baseline simulation with a moderately hard lure: 34% click, 11% submit credentials to the landing page, 6% report, median time-to-report 3 hours 40 minutes. The program: monthly five-minute role-based modules; a report button in the mail client with a thank-you reply within an hour; quarterly simulations of varied difficulty; finance and the help desk get callback and verification protocols as written procedures; passkeys rolled out to all staff in two quarters; DMARC moved from p=none to p=reject. Twelve months later with lures of the same difficulty: 7% click, 1% submit, 48% report, median time-to-report 9 minutes. The SOC now learns of a real campaign within minutes of delivery and quarantines it before the long tail opens. The number the CISO reports is not "click rate fell 79%" but "we detect and contain real phishing within ten minutes, and stolen passwords no longer work."</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"Smart people don't fall for this.\" Attacks target emotion and process, not intelligence; executives and security staff are routinely targeted and sometimes fooled, which is why controls must not depend on anyone being unfoolable.","\"A low click rate means we are safe.\" Click rate tracks lure difficulty; report rate, time-to-report and phishing-resistant MFA are what reduce real risk.","\"Phishing simulations should be as sneaky as possible.\" Cruel or unauthorized lures destroy trust and the reporting culture; simulations need authorization, policy-level consent and respectful design."],
problems:[
{q:"A finance clerk receives an email from the CEO's exact address asking for an urgent wire to a new vendor account before a 5 p.m. deadline, citing a confidential acquisition. List the three cues and the protocol step that neutralizes them.", a:"Authority (CEO), urgency (5 p.m.), process deviation (new account, confidentiality). Protocol: callback to the CEO's number on file and dual authorization; an authentic sender address proves nothing (spoofing or mailbox compromise)."},
{q:"Compute report-rate and time-to-report for a campaign: 500 delivered, 90 clicked, 160 reported, first report at 4 minutes, median 22 minutes. Interpret.", a:"Report rate 32%, click rate 18%, median time-to-report 22 min, first report 4 min. The SOC could quarantine within minutes of the first report; the clicks matter less if credentials are phishing-resistant. Target: raise reports above 40% and cut the median under 10 min."},
{q:"Your help desk resets MFA for anyone who gives name, employee ID and manager, all of which appear on LinkedIn and in the email directory. Redesign the check.", a:"Require something an outsider cannot assemble: a callback to the number on record, a video check with a known colleague or manager confirmation through an internal channel, a ticket opened from an authenticated device, and a waiting period for high-privilege accounts; log every re-enrollment."},
{q:"Write the five-line authorization memo for a phishing simulation.", a:"Sponsor and signatories (CISO, HR, legal); purpose and dates; scope and exclusions; rules (no real credential capture, no humiliating lures, data retention 90 days, aggregate reporting only); acknowledgment that staff were informed by policy."},
{q:"Explain why FIDO2 keys protected Cloudflare in 2022 when its staff did enter credentials on a phishing page.", a:"The key signs a challenge bound to the genuine site's origin; the fake page's origin does not match, so no valid assertion is produced and the stolen password alone cannot complete login."}
],
checks:[{q:"Seventh Cialdini principle added in 2016?",a:"Unity."},{q:"IC3 2025 figure for BEC losses?",a:"About 3.05 billion dollars (of roughly 20.9 billion total)."},{q:"Open source phishing-simulation platform named here?",a:"GoPhish."}],
explain:"Explain to ADA, with two BEC cases, why authority plus urgency plus a payment change is the signature of fraud, then design the verification protocol and the metrics you would report to a CISO instead of click rate."},
{id:"p32m7", title:"Reporting, Careers and the GOSI Exam", minutes:50,
outcomes:["Write an intelligence report in BLUF form with key judgments, confidence, sourcing and TLP marking.","Brief an executive in five minutes and survive the questions.","Define the metrics that prove an intelligence program earns its budget.","Describe the CTI analyst and OSINT investigator career paths, the budget credentials and the GIAC GOSI exam in detail.","Map GOSI's objectives to this curriculum and follow a twelve-week study plan."],
body:`<p>The question: you have run the cycle, collected legally, verified the images, mapped the actor; now how do you make someone act on it, prove the program was worth paying for, and turn the skill into a job? The answer is a report format, a briefing discipline, a handful of metrics and a credential plan.</p>
<h4>The report: BLUF first</h4>
<p>Intelligence writing is inverted: the conclusion comes first. <strong>BLUF</strong>, bottom line up front, is the opening paragraph that states the judgment, its confidence and what the reader should do; a busy executive who reads nothing else must still get the decision. A standard product then runs: <em>key judgments</em> (two to five sentences, each with <strong>estimative language</strong> and a <strong>confidence level</strong>); <em>scope and requirement</em> (which PIR this answers, the time window, what was excluded); <em>analysis</em> (the evidence and reasoning, alternatives considered via ACH, what would change the judgment); <em>sourcing</em> (every claim traceable to a graded source, Admiralty code in brackets); <em>recommendations</em> (specific, owned, dated); and <em>appendices</em> (IOCs as a STIX bundle or MISP event, ATT&CK mapping, timeline). Mark the whole document under TLP 2.0 (FIRST, 2022): TLP:RED for named recipients only, TLP:AMBER for the organization and its clients on a need-to-know basis (TLP:AMBER+STRICT for the organization alone), TLP:GREEN for the community, TLP:CLEAR for unlimited release. Dates in ISO 8601 with UTC, numbers with ranges, no adjectives doing the work of evidence.</p>
<pre>BLUF: It is very likely (high confidence) that the credential-stuffing attempts against
the customer portal on 2026-10-18 reused passwords from the September 2026 retail breach.
We recommend forcing resets for the 1,240 matching accounts and enabling passkeys by Q1.
KEY JUDGMENTS
1. Source IPs overlap 71% with a public stuffing botnet list [B2]; traffic began 14 h after
   the breach corpus appeared on a leak site [A1, Domain 47 monitoring].
2. We assess it is unlikely (moderate confidence) that the actor targeted us specifically...
TLP:AMBER  ·  Requirement PIR-3  ·  Window 2026-10-15 to 2026-10-22  ·  Analyst: T.M.</pre>
<h4>Briefing executives</h4>
<p>Five minutes, four parts: the bottom line and the ask (one sentence each); why you believe it (two pieces of evidence, graded); what happens if nothing is done (impact in their units: dollars, downtime, regulatory exposure); and what you need (a decision, a budget line, an owner). Pre-empt the three questions you will be asked: "how sure are you," answered with the confidence level and what would change it; "who else has this happened to," answered with a named public case; and "what does it cost," answered with a range. Never say "probably" without the percentage band behind it in your notes; never present an IOC list to a board.</p>
<h4>Metrics for an intelligence program</h4>
<table><tr><th>Metric</th><th>What it proves</th><th>Example target</th></tr>
<tr><td>Requirements coverage</td><td>Every product traces to a PIR; every PIR got a product this quarter</td><td>100% of active PIRs addressed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Timeliness</td><td>Time from public event (advisory, breach) to delivered assessment</td><td>Median under 48 hours</td></tr>
<tr><td>Decisions influenced</td><td>Count of changes (controls, patches, budget) traced to a report</td><td>Logged per report; reviewed monthly</td></tr>
<tr><td>Detections produced</td><td>Hunts and rules derived from TTP intelligence, with true positives</td><td>Rules deployed, alerts validated</td></tr>
<tr><td>Consumer feedback</td><td>Relevance and usability ratings; follow-up questions captured</td><td>Average above 4 of 5; questions become PIRs</td></tr>
<tr><td>Attack-surface trend</td><td>Unexpected internet-facing assets per quarter (Module 3)</td><td>Falling toward zero</td></tr>
<tr><td>Phishing resilience</td><td>Report rate and time-to-report (Module 6)</td><td>Above 40%; under 10 minutes</td></tr></table>
<h4>Careers</h4>
<p>Two paths share this domain. The <em>CTI analyst</em> sits beside a SOC (Domain 21) or in a vendor's research team: consuming feeds, mapping to ATT&CK, writing actor profiles and briefs, feeding detection engineering and red teams; the ladder runs from SOC analyst to CTI analyst to senior or lead analyst and threat researcher. The <em>OSINT investigator</em> works in corporate security and brand protection, trust and safety at platforms, due diligence and fraud at financial firms, journalism and human-rights organizations, law enforcement and intelligence as a civilian analyst, and missing-persons nonprofits such as Trace Labs; it also appears inside red teams as the reconnaissance specialist for authorized engagements (Domain 8). U.S. postings in 2026 commonly range from roughly 80,000 dollars for junior analysts to 150,000 or more for senior and lead roles, with vendor research and threat-hunting leads higher. The portfolio that gets interviews: a published threat-actor profile with ACH and sourcing, two or three geolocation write-ups from public practice sets, a MISP automation on GitHub, Trace Labs and OSINT Dojo badges, and a redacted attack-surface report on a fictional company.</p>
<h4>The credentials, honestly priced</h4>
<p>The GIAC Open Source Intelligence certification (<strong>GOSI</strong>) is the employer-recognized OSINT credential: 75 questions, 2 hours, 69% to pass, web-based and proctored through ProctorU or Pearson VUE, open-book (GIAC permits printed materials, so candidates build an index). The exam is 999 dollars whether bundled with training or bought alone, a practice test is 399, a retake 899, and renewal every four years costs 499 with 36 CPEs. Its course changed: SANS retired SEC487 (Micah Hoffman's "OSINT Gathering and Analysis"), its URLs now redirect to <em>SEC497: Practical Open-Source Intelligence</em> (Matt Edmondson), which is the course GIAC lists for GOSI, at 8,780 dollars OnDemand, six sections, 36 CPEs. The exam is affordable; the course is not, and self-study against the six published objectives is realistic. Budget tiers: EC-Council's CTIA (exam 312-85, about 550 dollars for e-courseware plus voucher; see Domain 47) covers the CTI framework side; TCM Security's Practical OSINT Research Professional (PORP, 399 dollars, a 72-hour practical exam with a written report, one free retake, the OSINT Fundamentals course included) proves hands-on skill at a tenth of the SANS price; and the free tier is real experience, not a badge: OSINT Dojo's ranked challenges (digital badges via Badgr), Trace Labs Search Party participation badges, and Gralhix's exercise write-ups.</p>
<table><tr><th>GOSI objective</th><th>Where it lives here</th></tr>
<tr><td>OSINT Methodology</td><td>Modules 1 and 2 (cycle, PIRs, documentation, passive vs active)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Privacy and Operational Security Fundamentals</td><td>Module 2 (analyst OPSEC, evidence, footprint lab)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Searching, Collecting, and Processing Data</td><td>Modules 3 and 4 (search operators, automation concepts, metadata, multimedia)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Network Data and Infrastructure Analysis</td><td>Module 3 (WHOIS/RDAP, passive DNS, CT, Shodan/Censys)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Investigating Businesses</td><td>Module 3 and the capstone (public and breach data about an organization)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Investigating People</td><td>Taught here defensively only: Modules 2 and 6 (your own footprint; how exposure enables pretexting). Practice on fictional personas and sanctioned sets.</td></tr></table>
<h4>A twelve-week plan</h4>
<p>Weeks 1–2: Modules 1–2, footprint lab, Heuer chapters on bias and ACH. Weeks 3–4: Module 3, attack-surface map of a fictional company, index pages for GOSI. Weeks 5–6: Module 4, twenty Gralhix and OSINT Dojo solves with case files. Weeks 7–8: Module 5, MISP in Docker, STIX bundle, one actor profile. Week 9: Module 6, awareness-program design. Week 10: capstone report with ACH. Weeks 11–12: GIAC practice test, index refinement, exam. Communities along the way: Trace Labs' Discord and Search Party events, OSINT Dojo, the archived OSINT Curious videos (the project closed in February 2023 but kept its library online), the SANS OSINT Summit and Cyber Threat Intelligence Summit (both February 1–2, 2027 in Alexandria, Virginia, 525 dollars in person), Bellingcat's published how-tos, and the Layer 8 and Social-Engineer podcasts for the human side.</p>
<p><em>Exam mapping.</em> This module is the exam itself: GOSI's six objectives mapped to lectures, the format and cost, the open-book index strategy, and the reporting standards (BLUF, estimative language, confidence, sourcing, TLP) that CTIA's dissemination domain and every employer's writing test demand.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>One page that changed a budget.</strong> BLUF: "It is very likely (high confidence) that our externally exposed remote-access portal is in scope for the ransomware affiliates active against regional contractors; we recommend moving it behind phishing-resistant MFA and the VPN by December 15 (owner: IT director; cost: 0 dollars in licensing, 40 engineering hours)." Key judgments cite three vendor reports (B1, B2, B2) describing the same initial-access technique, our own attack-surface map showing the portal (A1), and Have I Been Pwned domain results listing 212 corporate emails in breaches (A1). Alternatives considered: the affiliates may prefer VPN appliance exploits (unlikely, moderate confidence, no appliance exposed). What would change the judgment: a new public exploit for our portal's product. TLP:AMBER+STRICT. The executive asked two of the three predicted questions; the decision took eleven minutes, and the metric log records one decision influenced, one control changed, and a timeliness of 31 hours from the first vendor report.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"The report should build to the conclusion.\" Intelligence writing puts the bottom line first; the executive may read only that paragraph.","\"GOSI requires the SANS course.\" The exam is sold separately at 999 dollars; the course is optional and costs 8,780 dollars. Self-study against the published objectives is realistic.","\"OSINT jobs are about finding people.\" The paid roles are corporate exposure, fraud, trust and safety, brand protection, journalism and threat intelligence; investigating private individuals is the part of the field with the most legal and ethical constraints, not the career."],
problems:[
{q:"Rewrite this opening into a BLUF: 'After analyzing several sources over two weeks we noticed some indications that there might be interest in our sector from a group that has been active elsewhere, which we describe below.'", a:"BLUF: We assess it is likely (moderate confidence) that [group] has begun targeting our sector; two of three incidents since September match its ATT&CK profile. Recommend adding its techniques to the hunt plan this week (owner: SOC lead) and briefing the IR team."},
{q:"Choose the TLP 2.0 label for (a) a report naming a client's breached accounts, (b) a sector advisory for peer companies, (c) a blog post.", a:"(a) TLP:RED or TLP:AMBER+STRICT depending on recipients; (b) TLP:GREEN (or AMBER if limited to partners); (c) TLP:CLEAR."},
{q:"Total the first-year cost of GOSI self-study with one practice test and a pass, versus GOSI with SEC497 OnDemand. Then PORP.", a:"Self-study: 999 + 399 = 1,398 dollars. With SEC497: 8,780 + 999 (+399 practice) = 9,779–10,178 dollars. PORP: 399 dollars including the course and one retake."},
{q:"A CISO asks 'what did intel do for us this quarter?' Answer with three metrics and the number each needs.", a:"Decisions influenced (count of logged control changes traced to reports), timeliness (median hours from public event to assessment), detections produced with true positives; plus coverage of active PIRs. Give each with its quarter-over-quarter change."},
{q:"Design the index for an open-book GOSI attempt in one paragraph.", a:"Alphabetical terms with page references across your notes and the six objectives, tabs per objective, a one-page sheet of command syntax (exiftool, dig, crt.sh query, search operators) and a legal/OPSEC checklist; build it during study, test it on the practice exam."}
],
checks:[{q:"GOSI format?",a:"75 questions, 2 hours, 69% passing, proctored (ProctorU or Pearson VUE), 999 dollars."},{q:"Which SANS course now maps to GOSI?",a:"SEC497 Practical Open-Source Intelligence (SEC487 retired; its URLs redirect to SEC497)."},{q:"What does TLP:AMBER+STRICT mean?",a:"Sharing restricted to the recipient organization only, not its clients."}],
explain:"Dictate to ADA a one-page BLUF report on a fictional incident with key judgments, confidence, Admiralty-graded sources and a TLP label, then explain the GOSI cost picture and why self-study is realistic."}
],
listening:[],
labs:[
{id:"p32lab1", title:"Your digital footprint audit and cleanup plan", goal:"Experience exposure from the inside and practice evidence discipline on yourself.", steps:["Build an isolated research VM (Trace Labs OSINT VM or a Debian build) with a fresh browser profile; snapshot it.","Enumerate your own usernames, emails and photos across search engines, reverse image search and Have I Been Pwned; check EXIF on images you have posted (exiftool).","Record every finding as evidence: screenshot with UTC timestamp, URL, SHA-256, Admiralty grade, in a case file with your own PIR at the top ('What could an attacker use to pretext me?').","Write the cleanup plan in priority order (password rotation and passkeys first, then deletions, broker opt-outs, alerts) and execute the first five items; strip metadata from anything you keep online."], deliverable:"The case file (redacted), the prioritized plan with dates completed, and a paragraph on what surprised you."},
{id:"p32lab2", title:"Attack-surface map of an in-scope or fictional company", goal:"Produce a passive, people-free map a pentester and a defender can both use.", steps:["Pick an organization whose public bug-bounty policy explicitly permits reconnaissance of listed domains, or invent a fictional company and seed it with your own test domain; write the scoping statement (passive only, assets not people).","Enumerate from certificate transparency (crt.sh), passive DNS and web archives; deduplicate; note first-seen dates.","Search Shodan/Censys for the in-scope ranges or your own hosts only; search public code for the domain; run gitleaks on a repository you own; record the exposure metric (unexpected assets ÷ known assets).","Write the table with source and Admiralty grade per row and a reduction plan split into pentest-scope candidates (Domain 8) and blue-team actions (Domain 21)."], deliverable:"The asset table, the collection log, the exposure metric and the two-column action plan, with no individual named."},
{id:"p32lab3", title:"Geolocation and chronolocation challenge set", goal:"Solve twenty sanctioned challenges with documented reasoning.", steps:["Solve at least ten Gralhix OSINT exercises and five OSINT Dojo challenges; for each, log the clues, tools and confidence before checking the solution.","Take five photographs of public places yourself with GPS enabled; strip EXIF from copies and have ADA or a friend give them back unlabeled; geolocate and chronolocate them, then compare with the original EXIF as the answer key.","For two shadow problems compute elevation with tan(e) = h/L and confirm the time window in SunCalc; try Bellingcat's Shadow Finder for one unknown-location case.","Write a one-paragraph verification note for each solve in the ICD 203 style: judgment, confidence, what would change it."], deliverable:"Twenty case notes with accuracy scores, the two shadow computations, and your error analysis (which clue classes fooled you)."},
{id:"p32lab4", title:"MISP in Docker with a feed and a STIX bundle", goal:"Operate a threat-intelligence platform end to end on your own machine.", steps:["Clone the official misp-docker repository, configure .env, run docker compose up, and log in; enable the ATT&CK galaxy and the TLP taxonomy.","Enable one free default feed (for example an abuse.ch feed), fetch it, and measure: indicators pulled, how many correlate with a second feed, age distribution of the indicators.","Create an event from a public vendor report: attributes for its IOCs, tags for TLP and estimative language, galaxy clusters for its ATT&CK techniques; export it as STIX 2.1 and read the JSON.","Write a PyMISP script that lists all indicators added in the last 7 days tagged with one technique; optionally bring up OpenCTI and import the same bundle through its STIX import."], deliverable:"docker compose and configuration (secrets redacted), the feed-quality numbers, the exported STIX bundle and the PyMISP script."},
{id:"p32lab5", title:"Phishing-recognition drill and awareness-program design", goal:"Defend people with measurement, consent and respect.", steps:["Collect ten publicly documented phishing, vishing and BEC cases (vendor blogs, IC3 alerts, court records); for each, identify the persuasion principles, the process deviation and the control that would have stopped it.","Write the authorization memo and policy language for a simulation program at a fictional 600-person firm: sponsor, scope, exclusions, consent, data handling, lure rules.","Stand up GoPhish in your lab and send a simulation to accounts you own; record delivered/opened/clicked/reported and compute report rate and median time-to-report.","Design the twelve-month program: role-based modules, report button workflow, verification protocols for finance and the help desk, passkey rollout, DMARC enforcement, and the metrics dashboard."], deliverable:"The case analysis table, the memo and policy text, your GoPhish metrics, and the program plan with targets."}
],
capstone:{title:"A full intelligence report with ACH", brief:"Choose a publicly reported campaign or actor (criminal, state or hacktivist) and a fictional client in the telecom-contractor sector. Write the client's PIRs, run the cycle, and deliver a TLP-marked intelligence report: BLUF, key judgments with estimative language and confidence, an ACH matrix with at least three hypotheses on attribution or intent, Admiralty-graded sourcing from public reporting only, an ATT&CK mapping and Diamond Model, a people-free attack-surface summary of the fictional client, a social-engineering exposure assessment with verification protocols, and recommendations with owners and dates. Load the IOCs into your MISP instance and export the STIX bundle as an appendix. Brief it to ADA in five minutes.", rubric:["Requirements drive the product: PIRs stated, every section traces to one.","Analytic standards: estimative language and confidence used correctly, ACH matrix rejects on inconsistency, alternatives and change triggers stated.","Sourcing: every claim graded (Admiralty code) and reproducible; no non-public or stolen data; no private individual investigated.","Technical mapping: correct ATT&CK IDs, a coherent Diamond, a valid STIX 2.1 export from MISP.","Communication: BLUF first, TLP label, recommendations with owners and dates, a five-minute brief that survives the three predictable questions."]},
exam:[
{id:"p32q1", type:"mc", q:"The six stages of the intelligence cycle, in order:", options:["Collection, direction, analysis, processing, feedback, dissemination","Planning and direction, collection, processing, analysis and production, dissemination, feedback","Requirements, scanning, exploitation, reporting, remediation, retest","Observe, orient, decide, act, review, repeat"], answer:1, why:"Direction sets requirements, collection gathers, processing makes data usable, analysis produces judgments, dissemination delivers, feedback refines the requirements."},
{id:"p32q2", type:"mc", q:"In IC estimative language, 'likely' corresponds to:", options:["20–45%","45–55%","55–80%","80–95%"], answer:2, why:"Likely/probable is 55–80%; very likely is 80–95%; roughly even chance is 45–55%."},
{id:"p32q3", type:"mc", q:"In Analysis of Competing Hypotheses you reject hypotheses that:", options:["Have the least supporting evidence","Have the most inconsistent evidence","Were proposed last","Lack a named threat actor"], answer:1, why:"Consistent evidence often fits several hypotheses; disconfirming evidence is decisive, so ACH eliminates on inconsistency."},
{id:"p32q4", type:"mc", q:"Which activity is passive collection?", options:["Reading a company's certificates on crt.sh","Running nmap against hosts you found","Sending a connection request from a research account","Attempting a password reset on a subject's email"], answer:0, why:"Certificate transparency logs are public records already published; the other three touch the target or a person and are active (and the last two are forbidden in OSINT work)."},
{id:"p32q5", type:"mc", q:"Van Buren v. United States (2021) held that the CFAA's 'exceeds authorized access':", options:["Covers any violation of a website's terms of service","Applies to entering areas of a system one is not entitled to access, not misuse of accessible data","Makes all scraping illegal","Does not apply to federal employees"], answer:1, why:"The Court adopted the narrow 'gates-up-or-down' reading; ToS violations alone are not CFAA crimes."},
{id:"p32q6", type:"mc", q:"Under Trace Labs Search Party rules, a participant who likes a post by the missing person's sibling:", options:["Earns bonus points","Is immediately disqualified","Must notify law enforcement","May continue if the like is removed"], answer:1, why:"Contact with the subject, family or friends, including likes, tags and friend requests, means disqualification; collection is passive only."},
{id:"p32q7", type:"mc", q:"The richest passive source of a company's hostnames is usually:", options:["Its robots.txt","Certificate transparency logs","Its job postings","Port scans"], answer:1, why:"Chrome has required CT logging since 2018, so every public TLS certificate (and its hostnames) is in public logs; port scans are active."},
{id:"p32q8", type:"mc", q:"A defender wants to know which corporate addresses appear in breaches. The correct approach is:", options:["Download the breach dumps from a forum","Use Have I Been Pwned's domain search as the verified domain owner and force resets","Ask employees to search for themselves on criminal markets","Ignore it; breaches of other sites are irrelevant"], answer:1, why:"Possessing stolen credentials is the attacker's business; HIBP's domain search gives the exposure without handling the dumps."},
{id:"p32q9", type:"mc", q:"A 2.0 m post casts a 2.0 m shadow. The solar elevation is:", options:["30°","45°","60°","90°"], answer:1, why:"tan(e) = h/L = 1, so e = 45°."},
{id:"p32q10", type:"mc", q:"The absence of EXIF metadata in a social-media photo most likely means:", options:["The photo was faked","The platform stripped it on upload","The camera had no clock","The image is a screenshot"], answer:1, why:"Most major platforms strip EXIF routinely; absence is uninformative and presence is only a lead."},
{id:"p32q11", type:"mc", q:"On the Pyramid of Pain, the indicator type costing the adversary most to change is:", options:["Hash values","IP addresses","Network artifacts","TTPs"], answer:3, why:"Changing tactics, techniques and procedures means retraining the operation; hashes and IPs change in minutes."},
{id:"p32q12", type:"mc", q:"The four core features of the Diamond Model are:", options:["Threat, vulnerability, asset, impact","Adversary, capability, infrastructure, victim","Reconnaissance, delivery, exploitation, control","Source, method, target, result"], answer:1, why:"Caltagirone, Pendergast and Betz (2013); meta-features add timestamp, phase, result, direction, methodology and resources."},
{id:"p32q13", type:"mc", q:"STIX 2.1 is best described as:", options:["An HTTPS transport for feeds","A JSON language for expressing threat intelligence objects and relationships","A ransomware family","A SIEM query language"], answer:1, why:"STIX is the language; TAXII 2.1 is the HTTPS transport with API roots and collections."},
{id:"p32q14", type:"mc", q:"A feed delivers 60,000 indicators a day with a 2% false-positive rate. Alerting on all of them produces about:", options:["12 false alerts a day","120 false alerts a day","1,200 false alerts a day","12,000 false alerts a day"], answer:2, why:"60,000 × 0.02 = 1,200, which is why feeds are filtered to requirements and used for enrichment rather than raw alerting."},
{id:"p32q15", type:"mc", q:"The signature of business email compromise is:", options:["A malicious macro","Authority plus urgency plus a change to payment details outside the normal channel","A port scan from abroad","An expired certificate"], answer:1, why:"BEC uses no malware; the fix is callback verification to a number on file and dual authorization."},
{id:"p32q16", type:"mc", q:"The best operational metric for a phishing-awareness program is:", options:["Click rate","Number of training videos watched","Report rate and time-to-report","Number of simulations sent"], answer:2, why:"Click rate tracks lure difficulty; fast, frequent reporting lets the SOC contain real campaigns within minutes."},
{id:"p32q17", type:"sa", q:"Write the first paragraph (BLUF) of an intelligence report about a fictional credential-stuffing incident, including estimative language, a confidence level and a recommendation with an owner.", answer:"Example: 'It is very likely (high confidence) that the login attempts against the portal on 2026-10-18 reused passwords from the September retail breach, because source IPs overlap 71% with a public stuffing list [B2] and began 14 hours after the corpus appeared [A1]. We recommend the IT director force resets for the 1,240 matching accounts this week and enable passkeys by Q1.'", rubric:["Judgment stated first with a calibrated probability word","Confidence level separate from probability","Specific recommendation with owner and date; sourcing indicated"]},
{id:"p32q18", type:"sa", q:"A friend asks you to 'find out everything' about a person they are dating. Explain your refusal in terms of law, ethics and professional standing, and offer the one legitimate alternative.", answer:"Refuse: investigating a private individual without authorization fails necessity and proportionality, can constitute stalking or harassment under Texas Penal Code 42.07/42.072 regardless of data being public, violates platform ToS if research accounts are used, and would end a career in the field. Alternative: the friend may review what the person has chosen to share publicly themselves, and anyone with safety concerns should contact law enforcement or a licensed investigator operating under the law.", rubric:["Names the legal exposure (stalking/harassment statutes, ToS)","Applies the ethics tests (necessity, proportionality, no deception)","Refuses clearly and routes to a legitimate channel"]}
],
cards:[
{id:"p32c1", front:"Intelligence cycle", back:"Planning and direction → collection → processing → analysis and production → dissemination → feedback; a loop because feedback rewrites the requirements."},
{id:"p32c2", front:"PIR", back:"Priority intelligence requirement: a decision-maker's question phrased so it can be answered, bounded in population and time, and tied to a decision; decomposed into SIRs and a collection plan."},
{id:"p32c3", front:"ACH", back:"Analysis of Competing Hypotheses (Heuer): matrix of hypotheses vs evidence marked consistent/inconsistent; reject the hypotheses with the most inconsistencies, then check sensitivity and set milestones."},
{id:"p32c4", front:"Estimative language", back:"Calibrated probability words (ICD 203): almost no chance 1–5%, very unlikely 5–20%, unlikely 20–45%, roughly even 45–55%, likely 55–80%, very likely 80–95%, almost certain 95–99%; stated separately from confidence."},
{id:"p32c5", front:"OSINT", back:"Open-source intelligence: intelligence produced from publicly, lawfully available information collected against a requirement; excludes anything behind logins, deception or stolen data."},
{id:"p32c6", front:"Passive collection", back:"Collection that touches only third-party, already-published sources (search engines, CT logs, Shodan, archives); active collection touches the target or its people and needs written authorization."},
{id:"p32c7", front:"Sock puppet", back:"A research persona used to view platforms that hide content from logged-out visitors; usually a terms-of-service issue, illegal if it impersonates a real person, and never used to engage a private individual."},
{id:"p32c8", front:"Attack surface", back:"Everything of an organization visible from outside: domains, hosts, services, certificates, repositories, buckets, breached credentials; mapped passively and people-free, then reduced."},
{id:"p32c9", front:"Certificate transparency", back:"Public logs of every publicly trusted TLS certificate (required by Chrome since 2018); crt.sh queries like %.example.com reveal hostnames passively."},
{id:"p32c10", front:"EXIF", back:"Camera metadata in image files (make, model, timestamp, software, GPS); read with exiftool, stripped by most platforms, trivially forgeable, so a lead rather than proof."},
{id:"p32c11", front:"Chronolocation", back:"Estimating when an image was taken: shadows via tan(elevation) = height ÷ shadow length and SunCalc, plus vegetation, weather records, construction and upload metadata."},
{id:"p32c12", front:"IOC", back:"Indicator of compromise: an observable of one attack (hash, IP, domain, mutex); cheap to match and cheap for the adversary to change; bottom of the Pyramid of Pain."},
{id:"p32c13", front:"TTP", back:"Tactics, techniques and procedures: how an adversary operates, expressed as ATT&CK IDs; survives infrastructure changes and drives hunts and detections."},
{id:"p32c14", front:"Pyramid of Pain", back:"Bianco (2013): hashes → IPs → domains → network/host artifacts → tools → TTPs, ordered by how much detecting each costs the adversary."},
{id:"p32c15", front:"Diamond Model", back:"Caltagirone, Pendergast and Betz (2013): adversary, capability, infrastructure, victim as corners; meta-features; events chain into activity threads and cluster into activity groups."},
{id:"p32c16", front:"STIX", back:"OASIS STIX 2.1: JSON language of domain objects (indicator, malware, threat-actor, intrusion-set…), relationships, sightings and observables; moved over TAXII 2.1 collections."},
{id:"p32c17", front:"Pretexting", back:"A fabricated scenario that makes a harmful action feel normal; the engine inside phishing, vishing and BEC; defeated by processes with no urgency exception and callback verification."},
{id:"p32c18", front:"BLUF", back:"Bottom line up front: the report opens with the judgment, its confidence and the recommended action; followed by key judgments, analysis, graded sourcing and a TLP label."}
]
});
